[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] &gt;&gt; When I first started this channel, I talked a lot about electric cars. But it wasn\'t about like, you know, comparing models and which one is right for you or anything like that. I talked about electric cars in the same way that we always talk about like fusion energy, you know, it was it was something I'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'always wanted, but it just didn\'t quite exist yet. There were really only two options at the time and that was the Tesla Model S and the Model X and they were both just way out of my price range. So, the fact that today there are literally dozens of choices and every'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'single major manufacturer has at least one or two electric cars in their lineup. That\'s that\'s amazing to me. I love that. Obviously, I\'m all about that. But as any EV hater will point out, it does pose a few issues. Electric cars'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with their batteries and their permanent magnet synchronous motors, they do require resources that ice cars don\'t. Um and while the market is still maturing anyway, it\'s now starting to put strain on some of those resources. But what if they didn\'t?'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Researchers in Korea have developed what they call the impossible engine. Uh it\'s an electric motor that has no metal in it. So, let\'s talk about this. How did they do this? And what does that mean for our future? &gt;&gt; [music]'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; We\'ve been talking about carbon nanotubes and how they\'ll change the world since this channel started. Like we did a deep dive about them back in 2022, um but it\'s far from the earliest mention. And the reason we keep coming back to them is cuz they\'re they\'re kind of a miracle material. Like they\'re'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lighter and they\'re stronger than steel. They conduct electricity. Uh that\'s just the tip of the iceberg. So, why don\'t we see them everywhere? Why are we still talking about their potential and not what they\'re actually doing? Well, there\'s lots of reasons. The biggest'

[01:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one, probably the most obvious one, is that they\'re they\'re hard to make. Um production has ramped up quite a bit lately though. 15 and 1/2 tons of carbon nanotubes are expected to ship in 2026. Now, that might not sound like a lot, but these things are 50,000 times'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'smaller than a human hair. And thanks to this growth in production, they are starting to finally show up in products from transistors to medical devices and beyond. They haven\'t changed the world yet, but they\'re getting there. And one way that\'s happening is through'

[01:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the replacement of metals in some machines, which [music] can help decrease weight, cut down environmental damage. Like last year when a team at the Korean Institute of Science and Technology announced that they had built a working electric motor with zero metal windings. Let\'s talk about why this is a'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'good thing. Uh so first of all motors, let\'s let\'s let\'s step back a minute and talk about electric motors. At its core, you could say that an electric motor is basically, you know, exploiting the first law of thermodynamics. That\'s the one that says that energy isn\'t created'

[02:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or destroyed uh in an isolated system anyway. It only [music] changes form. So, an electric motor that changes from electricity to momentum. So, the thing that made my brain click to understand electric motors and how they work is to'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kind of start by talking about maglev trains. So, bear with me for just a second. I\'m sure you know how magnets work. If you align similar poles, say two north poles, they\'ll repel each other. Align opposite poles, say a north and south'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pole, they attract. Simple enough. So, on a maglev train, they line the bottom of the train with permanent magnets. And then the track is lined with rows of electromagnets. The beauty with electromagnets is you can flip the poles from north to south and vice versa by'

[03:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'changing the direction of the flow of electricity. So, while the north pole of the magnets on the train are aligned with the south poles of the electromagnets, they attract each other. But then they flip the polarity, which causes the train to move forward to the next one. Flip the polarities again, the'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'train moves forward again. Do this over and over in a synchronous pattern, and the train will propel itself forward. This is a major oversimplification, I know. I\'m just kind of explaining the concept here. But if you take this track system and fold it around on itself and'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make it into a circle, then you have an inner wheel, which is called the rotor, and an outer wheel, called the stator. And by synchronizing those polarities, the inner wheel will spin. And there\'s your electric motor. Okay, so now that we have that concept out of'

[03:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the way, let\'s narrow down to the electromagnet part of it, because that\'s where the nanotubes come in. The electromagnets are made out of coils of wire that are wound around tightly. Uh this is why they\'re called windings. And by the way, if you want to see what these windings look like when you tear a'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'motor apart, check out the channel BigStackD Casting. Uh it\'s a really fun channel. He basically takes motors and other machines and he breaks them down and he and he melts them. Uh it\'s it\'s deeply satisfying stuff. But you can see when he does this, like how much copper'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is in these motors and how much they wind around to create these electromagnets. So, copper is what\'s most often used because it\'s highly conductive, but it\'s relatively cheap compared to other metals. Like for example, aluminum is cheaper, uh but it\'s less conductive. Silver is more'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conductive, but it\'s less cheap. Copper gets used cuz it\'s kind of in that nice middle zone there. But because copper\'s used in so many applications from motors to air conditioners and so on, you know, they\'ve they\'ve kind of become a target'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for thieves. Uh it\'s that that resource scarcity I was talking about earlier. Real briefly, I do need to kind of go into a little side tangent about how conductivity works. So, at an extremely basic level, metals conduct by sharing'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'their electrons with other atoms. Atoms, as you know, have different shells of electrons around them, and metals that conduct have outer electrons on the outside that are easy to share. Now, you would think that that would mean that a metal with the most number of outer'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electrons would be the most conductive, but it doesn\'t really quite work that way. First of all, the electron levels aren\'t exactly like rungs on a ladder like you always see in the little, you know, cartoons and whatnot. Uh that\'s a very oversimplified way of looking at'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it. And that ladder structure is especially inaccurate when you\'re talking about solids cuz they\'re all packed together really tightly. Because electrons in solids actually act more like bands, and those bands can often overlap with each other. So, what makes'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something a good conductor is the ability for these electrons to jump between the bands. It\'s not just the outer electrons, it\'s the ability for them to jump to the outer level and then go share it across the other atoms. And in the case of copper, it has a lot of overlapping bands, which makes it a lot'

[05:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'easier for those electrons to travel around. That\'s why copper\'s such a good conductor. Got that? Okay. But the biggest downside of copper is the weight. Heavy windings make for heavy motors. More weight means more'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'energy needed to move that weight, which lessens the efficiency. So, yeah, depending on the design, copper winding is going to account for like 10 to 18% of the motor\'s weight. And for that reason, EV makers have actually experimented with aluminum windings. [music] It\'s lighter than copper, but as'

[05:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I said before, it\'s not as conductive as copper, so they have to pack in more windings to get the same amount of power. Uh the problem is there\'s only so much space in there. So, yeah, material scientists are always looking for something better to fill it with, and one of the most promising options has'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'always been carbon nanotubes. The reason is that carbon is four to five times lighter than copper. Raw carbon is also cheaper than copper and it\'s less attractive to, you know, low-tech thieves. Of course, none of that matters if the carbon doesn\'t conduct well, but'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as it turns out, it conducts just as well or even better under the right conditions. So, carbon comes in different forms. These are called allotropes. This guy knows what I\'m talking about. &gt;&gt; Superman, isn\'t it? &gt;&gt; Are you kidding? Is that What did Didn\'t Didn\'t you see us fly out of the sky? I'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was flying with my head. &gt;&gt; He\'s Superman. &gt;&gt; But we\'re the only two men who can fly. &gt;&gt; This is Superman. &gt;&gt; Just right. &gt;&gt; Now, Superman crushing a diamond out of coal isn\'t super realistic, but it does'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kind of show how allotropes of carbon can differ. Like coal and diamonds are useless for making magnets. The gap in their energy bands is just too big. But, it\'s a different story when it looks like this. So, graphite,'

[06:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or what we call pencil lead, is actually more conductive than copper when on a 2D plane, or 2D in the sense that material scientists use it. It\'s like how we say a layer of bricks is flat compared to the 3D wall. So, like that wall, the'

[07:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'graphite in the pencil is is is stacked in layers, you know, that\'s how it naturally occurs. And one single layer of graphite is called graphene. And electricity moves very fast across that single layer of graphene, a lot more'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'easily than if it was, you know, in multiple layers, cuz it\'s harder to jump across the layers. The problem is that wires aren\'t 2D. They\'re not a flat layer of atoms. They\'re a 3D structure. So, how do you get that 2D conductivity in a 3D space? Well, just like we curled'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up the maglev track to make a motor, you just kind of curl up that sheet of graphene, and you make a tube. A very small tube. A nano tube.'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Ha! Ha! He said it! He said it! &gt;&gt; You can see what I\'m talking about in this animation of a carbon nanotube. It only has one layer, which consists of rolled-up sheets of graphene. Now, this is amazing, obviously, and you can see how there\'s a lot of potential in carbon'

[08:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nanotubes, but as I said before, production is still a big issue. Also, expense. You need very sophisticated equipment to make these things. There are also some difficulties with handling cuz they\'re very fragile, and safety'

[08:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because, you know, you don\'t want to breathe this stuff in. But, the main reason is is technical. Um for them to be able to outwork copper as a conductor, they have to be pure, like perfectly pure. Pure as the blue sky. &gt;&gt; After all,'

[08:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how pure can pure be? Well, it can be pretty darn pure. &gt;&gt; So, most carbon nanotubes are just kind of too cluttered with impurities to conduct as well as they should. Remember, it\'s it\'s the ease of the electrons sliding over that layer that'

[08:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'makes them a good conductor, but they are tiny. They\'re They\'re at the atomic level, so yeah, any amount of impurities can gum up the works. So, scientists have been trying to figure out how to make clean carbon nanotubes for decades. And that paper from the Korean Institute'

[08:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of Science and Technology described a new way of doing this, and it\'s it\'s it\'s kind of groundbreaking. They use a technique that they call wet spinning. Uh now, all of this is kind of a little bit over my head, but basically, the carbon fibers are drawn through an acid'

[09:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bath, and that helps them align, and then they twist them together like yarn. They then wrap nine of these wires in an insulating film to basically make electric cables. And then, those cables were used as windings, you know, taking the place of copper. So, the wet'

[09:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spinning technique, it\'s new. It was kind of first discovered in 2001. Um it\'s slowly gained traction in the industry over time, and it\'s led to cheaper production of carbon nanotubes. And just to be clear, there there are other uh methods of producing them that'

[09:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have different advantages for other applications and whatnot, but for the highly conductive carbon nanotubes, wet spinning is kind of the top of the line. Now, to take it even further, the research team then used a special cleaning process to supercharge the'

[09:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conductivity. The process is called LAST, which stands for a lyotropic liquid crystal assisted surface texturing. Never going to get that right again. LAST basically changes the chemical properties of those impurities that I'

[09:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'talked about, and it kind of turns them into a film on the surface of the carbon, and then water just kind of strips that right off. And what\'s left are our carbon nanotubes in pristine condition, ready to carry a charge. And according to test by the team, LAST made'

[10:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the carbon nanotubes significantly more conductive. They started at a conductivity of 5.8 megasiemens per meter and improved it to 7.7 megasiemens per meter. That\'s like a 30% increase, basically 1/3 better. That\'s great. Uh now, let\'s get to the context. Aluminum'

[10:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is 35 megasiemens per meter, and copper is 58 megasiemens per meter. So, there\'s there\'s still a long way to go. But, copper weighs so much, and carbon nanotubes are so small that you can fit'

[10:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'way more of them in the windings and still have a lighter motor. And a lighter motor isn\'t just good for weight reasons and efficiency reasons, like I said before, it\'s also better for cooling reasons. You know, spinning a rotor generates heat, and a lighter motor means there\'s less mass to cool.'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, yeah, if carbon nanotubes could actually replace copper in these windings, motors will benefit across the board. And another benefit, by the way, is to the environment. According to the International Copper Association, the industry currently releases about 0.2%'

[10:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of global greenhouse gases. Now, that\'s a small part of a giant problem, but demand for copper is expected to double in the next 25 years. So, yeah, if we can meet at least some of that demand'

[11:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with carbon nanotubes, it would prevent an increase in pollution, and that could help save lives. Now, before people get in the comments and say, "So, yes, producing carbon nanotubes is energy intensive." Uh but, it\'s still cleaner than digging a bunch of rocks out of the'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ground. And, by the way, some carbon nanotube production methods are actually carbon negative, meaning they remove carbon from the atmosphere. In fact, this could be a use for captured CO2, and you know, maybe help create a carbon'

[11:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'economy that would help incentivize more carbon capture, which we all need. Now, we\'re pretty far from that. Definitely really far from that, but just shows another advantage of increased carbon nanotube demand. So, this is great. This is the future we'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want. We want to make this happen. What are the hurdles to this becoming a reality? We got to start with cost. That\'s the big one. So, even though we\'ve got improved new production methods, the carbon nanotubes typically sell for between 20 and 80 cents a gram. Compare that to the current price of copper,'

[11:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is under 1 cent per gram. Yeah, you can you can see the problem. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Now, carbon nanotube manufacturers and advocates and whatnot, they say that they\'re not yet producing at scale, which is fair. You know, it\'s comparing them to the metal industry is kind of'

[12:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like, I don\'t know, comparing a gourmet brioche to a loaf of Wonder Bread. And that\'s fair, again, but you know, that that high cost is what\'s kind of preventing all these nanotube projects from getting off the ground. Another reason is that they\'re still just not'

[12:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quite conductive enough. Now, despite coming up with that last process I was talking about that gets the carbon nanotubes so clean that Howie Mandel could lick it without flinching, the tech is still not quite ready for prime time. But still, this is amazing stuff. Just the fact that we\'re doing this is'

[12:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is amazing. The whole concept would have been laughable a decade ago. But yeah, this Korean team proved that an electric motor with carbon nanotube windings, they proved that this is possible. And here\'s the deal. They\'re not the only ones working on this. In April 2026,'

[12:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just a handful of months back, there was a team in Spain that was able to produce carbon nanotubes with a conductivity of 24.5 megasiemens per meter. That\'s literally three times higher than the carbon nanotubes created by the Korean'

[12:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'team. And that\'s approaching about half the conductivity of copper. The Spanish team is already working with some manufacturers to kind of stabilize this process, which involves adding aluminum trichloride to carbon nanotubes. So, I mean, yeah, after years of talking about'

[13:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'carbon nanotubes and and not really seeing anything happening, it\'s it\'s it\'s great to finally see some progress. They show a ton of potential to clean up and improve the technology that we use every day, like the technology behind motors, and the price is coming down, and the scaling is starting to happen.'

[13:25] [scene:talk]
